ile" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005939/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b766436967935033c945ef278fc467eb3cee6d379be2244fc5b6bacb12054cd" "basis" 1111 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005939/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-2504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $16{x}^{2}=(4x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=4x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+(4x)^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005939/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b766436967935033c945ef278fc467eb3cee6d379be2244fc5b6bacb12054cd" "basis" 1111 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005939/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-2504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005939/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c05a08d8162628e21f5cd0f4a253bb7a2733fb49041e999778716cebcb87e247" "basis" 1111 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/matha